PART 4 — CREDENTIAL REFERENCES IN AUTOMATION WORKFLOWS

Integrate the vault with the existing automation workflow system.

4.1 Workflow step model

Introduce a secure credential reference mechanism.

For example, a workflow may contain:

- Action: Fill credential.
- Credential reference ID.
- Target field locator.
- Field role: username or password.
- Expected website origin.

This is a conceptual example. Adapt it to the existing AutomationStep model.

The workflow must reference a saved credential rather than contain its actual value.

4.2 Recorder behavior

Preserve Phase 4A protections.

When recording a password field:

- Detect the password field.
- Record its locator and field role.
- Never capture its value.
- Never capture masked bullet characters as a substitute value.
- Never place the password into recorder memory or event payloads.
- Never create a credential automatically.

If a password field is detected, the HUD may show that the field requires a saved credential.

The user should be able to associate a saved credential later.

Do not automatically associate credentials based only on a similar domain or field name.

4.3 Workflow editor

When editing a workflow that contains an authentication field, provide an option to:

- Select an existing saved credential.
- Open the credential vault and create a new one.
- Leave the credential unassigned.

The selection must be explicit.

Display only safe credential metadata, such as the website and username identifier.

Never display the stored password.

If the selected credential does not match the workflow origin, show a warning and prevent unsafe automatic use.

